{
~A. 9sqrt(5)#This is not fully simplified. Factor the radicand using the greatest perfect-square factor, take the square root of that factor, and leave only the non-square factor under the radical.
=B. 3sqrt(5)#Correct. 45 \= 9 times 5, and sqrt(9) \= 3.
~C. 5sqrt(3)#This is not fully simplified. Factor the radicand using the greatest perfect-square factor, take the square root of that factor, and leave only the non-square factor under the radical.
~D. 15sqrt(3)#This is not fully simplified. Factor the radicand using the greatest perfect-square factor, take the square root of that factor, and leave only the non-square factor under the radical.
}


::ALG2_U03_L01_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L01_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8
Question:
Evaluate 27²⁄³.
{
~A. 6#This applies the rational exponent incorrectly. Use the denominator as the root index first, then apply the numerator as a power, and check by reversing the operation.
~B. 18#This applies the rational exponent incorrectly. Use the denominator as the root index first, then apply the numerator as a power, and check by reversing the operation.
=C. 9#Correct. The cube root of 27 is 3, and 3² \= 9.
~D. 3#This applies the rational exponent incorrectly. Use the denominator as the root index first, then apply the numerator as a power, and check by reversing the operation.
}


::ALG2_U03_L01_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L01_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8
Question:
Evaluate 81¹⁄⁴.
{
~A. 9#This applies the rational exponent incorrectly. Use the denominator as the root index first, then apply the numerator as a power, and check by reversing the operation.
~B. 4#This applies the rational exponent incorrectly. Use the denominator as the root index first, then apply the numerator as a power, and check by reversing the operation.
~C. 27#This applies the rational exponent incorrectly. Use the denominator as the root index first, then apply the numerator as a power, and check by reversing the operation.
=D. 3#Correct. The fourth root of 81 is 3 because 3⁴ \= 81, so the rational exponent asks for the number that multiplies as four equal factors.
}


::ALG2_U03_L01_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L01_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8
Question:
Rewrite a⁵⁄² in radical form.
{
=A. sqrt(a⁵)#Correct. The denominator 2 gives the square root, and the numerator 5 gives the power.
~B. fifthroot(a²)#This reverses or misuses the parts of the rational exponent. In aᵐ/n, n is the root index and m is the power on the radicand.
~C. 2a⁵#This reverses or misuses the parts of the rational exponent. In aᵐ/n, n is the root index and m is the power on the radicand.
~D. asqrt(5)#This reverses or misuses the parts of the rational exponent. In aᵐ/n, n is the root index and m is the power on the radicand.
}


::ALG2_U03_L01_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L01_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8
Question:
Rewrite cuberoot(x⁴) using a rational exponent.
{
~A. x³⁄⁴#This reverses or misuses the parts of the rational exponent. In aᵐ/n, n is the root index and m is the power on the radicand.
=B. x⁴⁄³#Correct. The index 3 is the denominator and the power 4 is the numerator.
~C. 4x³#This reverses or misuses the parts of the rational exponent. In aᵐ/n, n is the root index and m is the power on the radicand.
~D. x¹⁄⁴#This reverses or misuses the parts of the rational exponent. In aᵐ/n, n is the root index and m is the power on the radicand.
}


